        "title": "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "c960a70d-cb11-51ca-88d4-50c4fa7900ac",
        ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=5t-3,\\,y={t}^{4}+2t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=$ \n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=5,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=4{t}^{3}+2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4{t}^{3}+2}{5}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{4(1)^{3}+2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
